>> title: Shoyu Tare
>> category: Sauces & Gravies
>> tags: japanese, soy sauce, ramen, seasoning, make-ahead
>> counters: Ramen Shop
>> aka: shoyu tare, shōyu tare, 醤油ダレ, soy tare, ramen seasoning base, ramen tare, soy sauce base for ramen, kaeshi
>> pairs-with: shoyu-ramen, chashu, dashi
>> servings: 10
>> time: 8 hr 30 min
>> slack: forgiving — eight hours cold or overnight, and it is better after a week in the bottle; the alcohol has to be boiled off properly and the second steep held at 160°F, because a tare boiled with the kombu in it goes bitter and there is no straining that out
>> step.1: The broth is unseasoned; this is the two spoonfuls in the bottom of the bowl that make it shoyu ramen.
>> step.2: steep cold 8 hr
>> step.3: boil the alcohol off 2 min
>> step.4: warm to 160°F (70°C), steep 10 min
>> step.5: strain and bottle

The broth is unseasoned; this is the two spoonfuls in the bottom of the bowl that make it shoyu ramen.

Steep @kombu{1%piece}(4 in) and @dried shiitake{2}(whole) in @koikuchi soy sauce{1%cup}(240 mL; dark Japanese soy) in a jar in the fridge ~steep{8%hr}. Cold and slow is the point — heat pulls tannin out of the kombu and the shiitake goes muddy.

Boil @sake{1/4%cup}(60 mL), @mirin{1/4%cup}(60 mL) and @dark brown sugar{1%Tbs}(12 g) in a #saucepan{} ~boil{2%min}, until the raw alcohol smell has gone and the pan smells sweet.

Warm @&(~1)sweetened sake{} with @&(~2)steeped soy{} to 160°F (70°C), drop in @katsuobushi{1/2%cup}(5 g) and ~steep{10%min} off the heat. It must not boil: this is seasoning, not stock, and boiled soy sauce goes flat and harsh at the same time.

Strain @&(~1)tare{} through a fine sieve into a clean bottle. It keeps a month in the fridge and only improves in the first week. Two tablespoons in the bottom of a bowl, then the broth on top.
